From 719b8729a343767b5653b7b334b1abe2dd145703 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheCryptoDonkey <TheCryptoDonkey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 15:35:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: serve a public hostname's own certificate on app ports

An operator who points a domain at a node can now set public_host,
public_cert and public_key in /etc/archipelago/config.toml (or the
ARCHIPELAGO_PUBLIC_HOST/_CERT/_KEY environment variables). The app gate
then picks the certificate by SNI: a ClientHello for exactly the public
hostname gets that pair, and everything else (no SNI, IP addresses,
.local, onions, other names) keeps the node-CA leaf. Both pairs reload on
mtime, following symlinks, and both must pass the key/certificate pairing
check. The public certificate must also cover the hostname or it is not
served.

Renewal cannot swap two files at once, so while the public pair is
briefly missing or mismatched the last good public pair keeps serving the
public hostname; the node leaf is used for it only if no good public pair
has ever loaded. A public pair that keeps failing is retried at most
every 5 s rather than on every connection, and TLS handshakes on app
ports now time out after 10 s. With every setting unset, nothing served
changes.

Manifests gain a {{PUBLIC_HOST}} placeholder for derived_env, generated
files and generated certificates. It resolves to the public hostname, or
to the same value as {{HOST_IP}} when none is set, so manifests can use
it unconditionally. The manifest spec and developer guide list it.
