diff --git a/core/archipelago/src/appgate/tls.rs b/core/archipelago/src/appgate/tls.rs
index c0131f7..c28ed5c 100644
--- a/core/archipelago/src/appgate/tls.rs
+++ b/core/archipelago/src/appgate/tls.rs
@@ -32,13 +32,54 @@
 //! What IS logged at warn is a certificate that exists but cannot be parsed:
 //! that is a misconfiguration the operator can act on, and silently falling
 //! back to plain HTTP would hide it.
+//!
+//! # A public hostname gets its own certificate
+//!
+//! A node an operator has put on the internet under a domain (`public_host`
+//! in `/etc/archipelago/config.toml`) can carry a publicly trusted pair for
+//! that name — typically certbot's. Browsers on the internet do not trust the
+//! node CA, so for that one name the gate presents the public pair instead,
+//! chosen by the ClientHello's SNI (case-insensitive). Everything else — no
+//! SNI, an IP address (which TLS never sends as SNI), `.local`, an onion, any
+//! other name — gets the node-CA leaf exactly as before, so LAN, mesh and Tor
+//! access are untouched and the public pair is never offered for a name it
+//! was not configured for. It is also refused outright unless the certificate
+//! actually covers `public_host`.
+//!
+//! Both pairs are reloaded on mtime. `metadata` follows symlinks, so a pair
+//! that renewal swaps by repointing links is seen as changed the moment the
+//! links move.
+//!
+//! The files must be readable by the daemon's user (`archipelago`). certbot's
+//! own `/etc/letsencrypt/live/<host>/` is not: its directories are root-only.
+//! Have a certbot deploy hook copy the pair to, for example,
+//! `/etc/archipelago/ssl/public/{fullchain,privkey}.pem`, owned
+//! `root:archipelago` with mode `0640`, writing each to a temporary file and
+//! `mv`-ing it into place so the gate never reads half a PEM.
+//!
+//! The two pairs fail independently. Renewal cannot swap two files at once,
+//! so a reload can briefly find the public pair missing or mismatched; the
+//! last good public pair then keeps serving `public_host`, with a warning,
+//! until a sound pair appears. Only a node that has never loaded a good
+//! public pair serves the node leaf for `public_host`. A public pair that
+//! keeps failing to load is retried at most every `PUBLIC_RETRY`, not on every
+//! connection, because these ports face the internet and a handshake must not
+//! buy an unauthenticated caller four file reads and two signature checks.
+//!
+//! A node with a public pair but no node leaf answers TLS for `public_host`
+//! and refuses the handshake for any other name, which a browser reports as a
+//! connection failure — the same outcome as a TLS connection to a node with no
+//! certificate at all today. Plain HTTP is decided by the first byte, before
+//! any of this, so it is unaffected either way.
 
